1
00:00:04,350 --> 00:00:07,066
For twenty years, risk models were built in R.

2
00:00:07,066 --> 00:00:10,562
A statistician writes the script, runs it on their own laptop, and

3
00:00:10,562 --> 00:00:11,568
emails the numbers.

4
00:00:11,568 --> 00:00:13,782
It works, until that person leaves.

5
00:00:15,850 --> 00:00:20,383
R runs in memory, one thread at a time, and it's hard to plug into production.

6
00:00:20,383 --> 00:00:23,774
Meanwhile, fifty-eight percent of developers use Python.

7
00:00:23,774 --> 00:00:24,376
R?

8
00:00:24,376 --> 00:00:25,024
Five.

9
00:00:26,850 --> 00:00:28,723
So why R, and not SAS?

10
00:00:28,723 --> 00:00:33,221
SAS migration is already industrialised: EXL's own Code Harbor cut

11
00:00:33,221 --> 00:00:35,607
conversion time by seventy percent.

12
00:00:35,607 --> 00:00:40,758
R is the gap: twenty-two thousand open packages, each with its own defaults.

13
00:00:42,850 --> 00:00:44,918
And those defaults are the trap.

14
00:00:44,918 --> 00:00:50,230
Under PRA SS1/23, a rewrite is a model change that needs evidence.

15
00:00:50,230 --> 00:00:54,073
One hidden penalty or missing-value rule, and a coefficient shifts

16
00:00:54,073 --> 00:00:55,878
enough to flip a loan decision.

17
00:00:57,850 --> 00:01:02,166
Meet R-Pythonizer: a web platform that moves statistical models from R

18
00:01:02,166 --> 00:01:04,200
to Python, and proves the result.

19
00:01:04,200 --> 00:01:05,531
The rule is simple.

20
00:01:05,531 --> 00:01:07,198
AI writes the Python.

21
00:01:07,198 --> 00:01:08,714
Plain code proves it.

22
00:01:08,714 --> 00:01:10,153
A human approves.

23
00:01:10,153 --> 00:01:14,975
And if a model has no faithful Python equivalent, the platform says so and stops.

24
00:01:16,850 --> 00:01:19,994
R runs the model once and freezes an answer key.

25
00:01:19,994 --> 00:01:24,925
Python gets R's exact rows, and the AI never sees R's numbers, so it can't cheat.

26
00:01:24,925 --> 00:01:29,091
Then plain code checks every coefficient and prediction, and if anything

27
00:01:29,091 --> 00:01:30,827
fails, an agent finds out why.

28
00:01:33,350 --> 00:01:37,006
But a perfect copy of a bad model is still a bad model, so we also check

29
00:01:37,006 --> 00:01:40,001
calibration, stability and fairness, as a separate verdict.

30
00:01:42,350 --> 00:01:46,808
Seven fixed steps, four narrow agents, zero data rows, and a script

31
00:01:46,808 --> 00:01:48,006
anyone can re-run.

32
00:01:48,006 --> 00:01:52,914
We tried four AI models, and GPT-6.1 terra, fast and cheap, got the same

33
00:01:52,914 --> 00:01:55,436
verified result for under five cents.

34
00:02:00,550 --> 00:02:04,506
Let's try it on a real credit-default model: an R notebook and a hundred

35
00:02:04,506 --> 00:02:05,824
and fifty thousand rows.

36
00:02:07,650 --> 00:02:11,589
The Analyst agent drafts the spec, and even flags what's missing, like a

37
00:02:11,589 --> 00:02:12,738
labelled holdout set.

38
00:02:15,150 --> 00:02:17,588
R runs once and freezes the answer key.

39
00:02:19,250 --> 00:02:23,289
The Translator writes Python, and twenty-five checks pass: coefficients

40
00:02:23,289 --> 00:02:26,190
and predictions match R to thirteen decimal places.

41
00:02:26,190 --> 00:02:27,510
Business fitness?

42
00:02:27,510 --> 00:02:30,982
Not established yet, and the platform tells you exactly why.

43
00:02:32,250 --> 00:02:36,899
The report backs every number with evidence, and the package re-runs the proof anywhere.

44
00:02:40,550 --> 00:02:41,680
R-Pythonizer.

45
00:02:41,680 --> 00:02:44,354
AI writes, code proves, humans approve.

